// metajs-rt.metajs -- MetaJS'S OWN LAYER 2, and the door Part B was blocked on.
//
// Every other language already had this file. MetaJS did not, because MetaJS *was*
// the bootstrap: languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf linked exactly one runtime input,
//
//     if (rts == undefined || rts.length == 0) { rts = [runtimePath()] }
//
// lib/runtime.ll - the C floor and nothing else. docs/runtime-next-plan.md
// ("Part B, move 1: `case_map` CANNOT MOVE") measured what that costs: a floor body
// that moves out of C and into languages/lib/runtime.metajs arrives in the other
// fifteen languages' `-exe` builds through their `<lang>-rt.ll`, and DISAPPEARS from
// MetaJS's. Since every remaining Part B candidate is reached through the floor's
// host-builtin dispatch - which IS MetaJS's standard library, 46 of `case_map`'s 114
// corpus calls being MetaJS's own two programs - Part B's movable set was empty.
//
// This file is the missing half. It is deliberately THIN: it imports
// lib/runtime.metajs and exports nothing of its own, because MetaJS's semantics are
// not a target language's - the metajs compiler emits the generic js_* externs and
// the C floor answers all of them. What the import buys is the DOOR: once
// `metajs-rt.ll` is linked next to `runtime.ll`, a body that leaves runtime.c for
// runtime.metajs is reachable from all SIXTEEN native builds instead of fifteen, and
// the floor half of the pair (a `js_*` upcall declared in runtime.c) resolves in
// every link rather than in every link but one.
//
// Compile with:
//     mec languages/metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf languages/lib/metajs-rt.metajs -q -rt-lib
// which is what tests/gen-metajs-rt-ll.sh does; the result is the checked-in
// languages/lib/metajs-rt.ll that metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf links next to lib/runtime.ll
// when it builds an -exe. An explicit `-rt` REPLACES both, which is what keeps
// tests/metajs-link-stubrt.c standing in for the whole runtime in the link tests.
//
// -rt-lib exports every TOP-LEVEL `function js_*` of this file AND OF EVERY FILE IT
// IMPORTS (metajs-to-llvm-ir.abnf, the -rt-lib block; the scan was widened when a
// `function js_jadd` written in runtime.metajs compiled, ran correctly under
// llvm.Run, and was undefined at link time). So the seven exports of runtime.metajs -
// js_jadd, js_is_type, js_dict_new, js_char, js_char_code, js_supercall, js_pyset -
// are what this module currently offers. NONE of them is emitted by the metajs
// compiler and none collides with a runtime.c definition; they are the shared
// layer-2 surface of the OTHER languages, riding along because the import is the
// point. When a floor body moves into runtime.metajs, it joins them here for free.
//
// THE ONE RULE FOR ADDING TO THIS FILE: a body that belongs to more than one
// language goes in lib/runtime.metajs, not here. This file is for what is MetaJS's
// alone - and so far that is nothing.
//
// The frozen-subset rules of every MetaJS source apply
// (docs/abnf-dialect-gotchas.md): no ASI, so every `return` carries its value on the
// same line, and a value-less early return is braced.

// The shared layer-2 runtime (docs/runtime-merge-plan.md part A). Imported, not
// copied: this is the same text the other fifteen `<lang>-rt.metajs` files import,
// and the import is the whole reason this file exists.
import "./runtime.metajs"

// MetaJS's own exports go here. There are none yet, and that is the correct state:
// see the header. The import above supplies the export surface -rt-lib requires.
